# BSidesSF 2020 CTF – CSP Two Challenge (AngularJS Bypass)

## Overview

This challenge from the **BSidesSF 2020 CTF** demonstrates an advanced **Content Security Policy (CSP) bypass** technique using trusted domain exploitation and AngularJS template injection. Unlike simpler CSP bypasses that rely on `data:` URLs, this challenge requires leveraging whitelisted external domains and framework vulnerabilities.

The primary learning goal is understanding how seemingly secure CSP configurations that whitelist trusted domains can still be exploited through sophisticated client-side attacks.

## Challenge Description

The web application implements a restrictive CSP policy that blocks most external resources but makes an exception for Google's AJAX libraries CDN:

```http
Content-Security-Policy: connect-src '*'; default-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; script-src 'self' ajax.googleapis.com 'unsafe-eval'
```

The challenge requires bypassing this CSP to execute arbitrary JavaScript and potentially exfiltrate sensitive data from the application.

## CSP Policy Analysis

### Policy Breakdown

- **`script-src 'self' ajax.googleapis.com 'unsafe-eval'`**:
  - Allows scripts from the same origin (`'self'`)
  - Permits loading scripts from `ajax.googleapis.com` (Google's CDN)
  - Enables `eval()` and similar dynamic code execution methods (`'unsafe-eval'`)
  
- **`default-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'`**:
  - Sets default policy to same-origin with inline content allowed
  - Applies to resources not explicitly covered by other directives
  
- **`connect-src '*'`**:
  - Allows `fetch`, `XMLHttpRequest`, and WebSocket connections to any domain
  - Creates an avenue for data exfiltration

### Security Assessment

This CSP configuration creates several attack vectors:

1. **Trusted Domain Exploitation**: `ajax.googleapis.com` hosts numerous JavaScript libraries, including vulnerable versions
2. **Dynamic Code Execution**: `unsafe-eval` enables runtime JavaScript generation
3. **Data Exfiltration Path**: Wildcard `connect-src` allows sending data anywhere
4. **Framework Vulnerabilities**: Older library versions may contain exploitable features

---

## Attack Vector: AngularJS Template Injection

## Technical Deep Dive

### Payload Component Analysis

#### 1. Primary AngularJS Loading
```html
<script src="//ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/angularjs/1.1.3/angular.min.js"></script>
```
- Loads AngularJS 1.1.3, which contains template injection vulnerabilities
- Uses protocol-relative URL to match current scheme
- Satisfies CSP requirement for `ajax.googleapis.com` domain

#### 2. Application Context Setup
```html
<div ng-app ng-csp>
```
- `ng-app`: Creates an AngularJS application scope
- `ng-csp`: Tells AngularJS to operate in CSP-compatible mode
- Enables template processing within the div

#### 3. Base URL Manipulation
```html
<base href=//ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/>
```
- Sets the base URL for relative script sources
- Simplifies loading additional libraries from the same CDN
- All subsequent relative URLs resolve against this base

#### 4. Additional Library Loading
```html
<script src=angularjs/1.0.1/angular.js></script>
<script src=prototype/1.7.2.0/prototype.js></script>
```
- Loads an even older AngularJS version (1.0.1) with more vulnerabilities
- Loads Prototype.js 1.7.2.0 which adds the `curry` method to Function.prototype
- Both libraries load from the trusted domain via the base URL

#### 5. Template Injection Execution
```html
{{$on.curry.call().alert('test')}}
```
- `$on`: AngularJS scope method for event handling
- `.curry`: Method added by Prototype.js to all functions
- `.call()`: Executes the curried function
- `.alert('test')`: Demonstrates arbitrary code execution

### Why This Attack Works

#### AngularJS Template System
- AngularJS evaluates expressions within `{{ }}` in the context of the current scope
- Early versions had insufficient sandboxing for template expressions
- The `ng-csp` directive doesn't prevent template evaluation, only inline script execution

#### Prototype Pollution Chain
1. Prototype.js extends `Function.prototype` with additional methods
2. The `curry` method creates a new function that can be called later
3. AngularJS scope methods like `$on` inherit these prototype extensions
4. Method chaining allows building complex execution chains

#### CSP Compliance
- All scripts load from the whitelisted `ajax.googleapis.com` domain
- No inline scripts are used (satisfies CSP)
- Dynamic code execution happens through template evaluation (allowed by `unsafe-eval`)

### 1. Identify Vulnerable Versions
- **AngularJS 1.0.x - 1.1.x**: Known template injection vulnerabilities
- **Prototype.js 1.7.x**: Adds exploitable prototype methods
- Test combinations for maximum effectiveness

### 2. Craft the Basic Exploit
```html
<!-- Minimal working exploit -->
<script src="//ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/angularjs/1.1.3/angular.min.js"></script><div ng-app ng-csp><base href=//ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/><script src=angularjs/1.0.1/angular.js></script><script src=prototype/1.7.2.0/prototype.js></script>{{$on.curry.call().alert(test)}}>
```